Looking at the code, I need to:
1. Add a `check_mode` flag
2. Parse `-c` / `--check`
3. In check mode, walk adjacent pairs using the existing `compare_lines` and report the first violation

Here are the changes to `src/new_sort/new_sort.c`:

**1. Add the `check_mode` static variable** (after `static int unique_mode = 0;`):

```c
static int check_mode = 0;
```

**2. Add `--check` to the long-option parsing** (after the `--unique` block, before the short-option cluster block):

```c
} else if (strcmp(arg, "--check") == 0) {
    check_mode = 1;
```

**3. Add `c` to the short-option cluster loop** (after the `*p == 'u'` branch):

```c
} else if (*p == 'c') {
    check_mode = 1;
```

**4. Insert the check-mode block** between the input-reading section (after `free(buf);`) and the existing sort section (`if (line_count > 1) {`). This block should come right after the `if (buf_len > 0) { add_line(...); } free(buf);` section:

```c
    if (check_mode) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i + 1 < line_count; i++) {
            int cmp = compare_lines(&lines[i], &lines[i + 1]);
            if (cmp > 0 || (cmp == 0 && unique_mode)) {
                size_t lineno = i + 2; /* 1-based line number of the out-of-order line */
                size_t n = lines[i + 1].len;
                fprintf(stderr, "new_sort: -:%zu: disorder: ", lineno);
                if (n > 0) {
                    fwrite(lines[i + 1].data, 1, n, stderr);
                }
                fputc('\n', stderr);
                cleanup();
                return 1;
            }
        }
        cleanup();
        return 0;
    }
```

